# Should big tech be broken up?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is antitrust for — prices, competition, or power? Why do economists disagree about platforms that are free to users?"
next: {"title":"Why does nobody pay for the pollution?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/42-why-does-nobody-pay-for-the-pollution.cast"}

## A monopoly that costs nothing
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Google
      - All others
    values:
      - 89
      - 11
    value_labels: true
    colors:
      - red
      - grey
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_label: '% of US searches, 2020'
    label_size: 26
    gap: 0.45
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 170
      w: 430
      h: 440
sources:
    - id: mehta2024
      title: United States v. Google LLC, Memorandum Opinion (D.D.C., 5 August 2024)
      authors: Judge Amit P. Mehta
      year: 2024
      finding: Google is a monopolist in general search services and unlawfully maintained that monopoly (Sherman Act §2), chiefly through exclusive default agreements; 89.2% share of general search queries in 2020 (94.9% on mobile); $26.3 billion paid for default placements in 2021.
    - id: bork1978
      title: 'The Antitrust Paradox: A Policy at War with Itself'
      authors: Robert H. Bork
      year: 1978
      finding: Argues that the only legitimate goal of antitrust is consumer welfare; the book shaped the US consumer-welfare standard.
    - id: khan2017
      title: Amazon's Antitrust Paradox
      authors: Lina M. Khan
      year: 2017
      finding: 'Yale Law Journal 126(3): argues that a consumer-welfare standard focused on short-run prices misses the market power of platforms such as Amazon; calls for attention to market structure and power.'

    text zero "$0" #2f6b8f x 760 y 545 font_size 110 hidden true
    text zero_sub "a search" #2f6b8f x 760 y 465 font_size 34 hidden true
    text stamp "MONOPOLIST (2024)" #b5482e cites ["mehta2024"] x 760 y 385 font_size 36 hidden true
    scale paid cites ["mehta2024"] x 560 y 250 width 400 label "paid for defaults, 2021 ($ billion)" unit "" min 0 max 50 value 26.3 ticks 5 hidden true
    text bn "$26bn" #b5482e cites ["mehta2024"] x 760 y 245 font_size 72 hidden true
    box g_prices "Prices" width 300 height 110 radius 14 icon "price tag" icon_side left font_size 34 in goals hidden true
    box g_comp "Competition" width 300 height 110 radius 14 icon ["person running","running","checkered flag"] icon_side left font_size 34 in goals hidden true
    box g_power "Power" width 300 height 110 radius 14 icon crown icon_side left font_size 34 in goals hidden true
    column goals gap 40 fit.h 400 fit.w 420 fit.x 90 fit.y 240 hidden true
    text v_prices "no harm" right g_prices gap 30 #8f887c font_size 36 hidden true
    text v_comp "harm?" right g_comp gap 30 #b5482e font_size 36 hidden true
    text v_power "harm?" right g_power gap 30 #b5482e font_size 36 hidden true
    text bork "Robert Bork, 1978:\n'consumer welfare'" #3d3833 cites ["bork1978"] x 790 y 440 font_size 38 hidden true
    portrait khan cites ["khan2017"] x 830 y 440 width 190 of "Lina Khan" cameo true hidden true

    card title "Should big tech be broken up?"

Should big tech be broken up? Start with Google search: in 2020 it handled about nine in every ten searches in America.
    draw axes bar_1 bar_2

B: And the price of a  (@draw zero zero_sub@)search? Zero. Nobody has ever paid for one.

Yet in 2024 a US judge ruled that Google i (@draw stamp@)s a monopolist, and had broken the law to stay one.

B: Not by charging too much. By paying Apple, Samsung and others to make it the default search engine.
    highlight bar_1 effect box

    draw paid

    ask "Click the line where you think it lies." on paid store g default "5" relative true voice b
        intro: So guess: how much did Google pay for those default spots, in 2021 alone?
        right: Close: you said {g} billion. It was {g.true} billion.
        wrong: You said {g} billion. It was {g.true} billion.

    erase paid

Twenty (@draw bn@)-six billion dollars, to keep giving away something free.

B: Then where's the harm? The textbook monopolist hurts you with a high price, and here the price is zero.
    highlight zero zero_sub effect box

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 zero zero_sub stamp bn

    card title "What is antitrust for?"

That puzzle splits the breakup d (@draw g_prices g_comp g_power parallel true@)ebate, because it hangs on what antitrust is for.

One answer: low prices for consumers. Robert B (@draw bork@)ork's 1978 book made that the American test for decades.
    highlight g_prices effect box

    erase bork

B: A second: protect the race itself, so a newcomer can still enter and win, whatever today's prices.
    highlight g_comp effect box

And the oldest: limit private power for its own sake. Lina  (@draw khan@)Khan revived that view in 2017, aiming at Amazon.
    highlight g_power effect box

    erase khan

B: By the price te (@draw v_prices@)st, Google harms nobody. By the other t (@draw v_comp@)wo, mayb (@draw v_power@)e a great deal.

    quiz "What should antitrust protect first?" judge false intro "Your view first."
        right: Hold on to it. Free platforms are exactly where these answers come apart.
        * Low prices
        * Open competition
        * Limits on power

    pause 0.3

## Two sides, one price
vars:
    'n':
      value: 0.2
      min: 0
      max: 0.8
      step: 0.05
    V1:
      expr: 10 + 100*n
    V2:
      expr: 30 + 50*n
sources:
    - id: rochet2003
      title: Platform Competition in Two-Sided Markets
      authors: Jean-Charles Rochet and Jean Tirole
      year: 2003
      finding: 'Journal of the European Economic Association 1(4): a platform''s price structure across its two sides matters, and the profit-maximising price to one side can be at or below cost when that side attracts the other.'

    box app "FishMarket" x 500 y 430 width 270 height 110 radius 16 icon ["mobile phone","smartphone"] icon_side left font_size 32 hidden true
    icon buyers size 110 set twemoji x 160 y 450 or ["shopping bags","shopper"] icon_look picture of "shopping cart" hidden true
