    text fishers_lbl "Fishers" x 840 y 365 font_size 34 hidden true
    text fishers_price "$30 a day" #b5482e x 840 y 290 font_size 48 hidden true
    arrow link_b #8f887c from.anchor right from.ref buyers to.anchor left to.ref app hidden true
    arrow link_f #8f887c from.anchor left from.ref fishers to.anchor right to.ref app hidden true
    arrow net curved #d0865f from.anchor top from.ref buyers to.anchor top to.ref fishers hidden true
    text net_lbl "more buyers, more value" #d0865f x 500 y 615 font_size 32 hidden true
    text two_sided "two-sided platform" cites ["rochet2003"] x 500 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true
    text squeeze_f "fee ↑" #b5482e x 840 y 215 font_size 38 hidden true
    text squeeze_b "ads ↑  privacy ↓" #b5482e x 160 y 215 font_size 32 hidden true
    path base #3d3833 points [[170,250],[760,250]] hidden true
    shape bar1 #b5482e shape rect x 330 y 236 width 220 height 72 bind.height 4*V1 bind.y "250 + 4*V1/2" style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.8 hidden true
    text val1 "${V1:0}" x 330 y 300 font_size 44 bind.y "250 + 4*V1 + 30" hidden true
    text lbl1 "One app\nfee $30" x 320 y 205 font_size 34 hidden true
    shape bar2 #2f6b8f shape rect x 600 y 272 width 220 height 144 bind.height 4*V2 bind.y "250 + 4*V2/2" style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.8 hidden true
    text val2 "${V2:0}" x 600 y 374 font_size 44 bind.y "250 + 4*V2 + 30" hidden true
    text lbl2 "Two apps\nfee $10" x 590 y 205 font_size 34 hidden true
    text unit "a fisher's day,\nafter the fee" #8f887c x 870 y 300 font_size 30 hidden true
    text nread "network effect\nn = {n:2}" #d0865f x 870 y 470 font_size 36 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"\\text{One app: } 40 + 100n - 30"},{"tex":"\\text{Two apps: } 40 + 50n - 10"},{"tex":"50n = 20 \\Rightarrow n = 0.4"}] x 500 y 420 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Two sides, one price"

B: A zero price blinds the price test. To see why a monopolist would c (@draw app@)harge zero, build a platform in Harbourtown.

B: Say, with illustrative numbers, there's one (@draw buyers buyers_lbl link_b buyers_price@) app where the town's fishers sell their catch. Buy (@draw fishers fishers_lbl link_f fishers_price@)ers use it free; fishers pay thirty dollars a day.

Why give one side the app for nothing?
    highlight buyers_price effect circle

B: Because the buyers are what the fis (@draw net net_lbl@)hers are paying for. Every extra buyer makes the app worth more to every fisher.

B: That's a two (@draw two_sided@)-sided platform. Charging one side nothing, even below cost, can be the most profitable price there is.

    erase two_sided

So zero isn't proof of no power. Where would the power show up?
    highlight app effect box

B: In the fishers'  (@draw squeeze_f@)fee, which ends up in the price of fish. Or on the fre (@draw squeeze_b@)e side, as more ads and less privacy.

    erase squeeze_f squeeze_b net net_lbl

    erase app buyers buyers_lbl buyers_price fishers fishers_lbl fishers_price link_b link_f

Then split it: two apps (@draw base lbl1 lbl2@), competing the fee down to, say, ten dollars a day.

B: But each reaches only half the buyers. Here's w (@draw unit bar1 val1 bar2 val2@)hat one fisher gets from the app each day, after the fee.

    draw nread

With a weak network effect, two apps win: forty dollars a day against thirty.
    highlight bar2

B: Try it: drag the network effect up, and find where one big app beats two small ones.
    explore params ["n"]

Past that point, cutting the network costs more than competition saves. That's the quarrel about breakups, in one dial.
    animate n 0.6 duration 2.5

    pause 0.4

    fade base lbl1 lbl2 unit bar1 val1 bar2 val2 nread to 0.15

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" intro "Want the dial's numbers?" on mathq stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

One app: forty dollars of convenience, plus a hundred buyers times n, the network effect, minus the thirty-dollar fee.
    draw math_box math_line_1

Two apps: forty, plus fifty buyers times n, minus ten.
    draw math_line_2

They're equal when fifty n equals twenty: at n of zero point four.
    draw math_line_3

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    fade base lbl1 lbl2 unit bar1 val1 bar2 val2 nread to 1

    pause 0.3

## Break up, or open up?
vars:
    'n':
      value: 0.6
      fixed: true
    h:
      value: 0.5
      min: 0.5
      max: 1
    V1:
      expr: 10 + 100*n
    V2:
      expr: 30 + 100*h*n
sources:
    - id: dma
      title: Regulation (EU) 2022/1925 on contestable and fair markets in the digital sector (Digital Markets Act)
      authors: European Parliament and Council of the EU
      year: 2022
      finding: Designated gatekeeper platforms must meet obligations from March 2024, including interoperability duties and a ban on self-preferencing their own services.
    - id: boasberg2025
      title: Federal Trade Commission v. Meta Platforms, Inc., Memorandum Opinion (D.D.C., 18 November 2025)
      authors: Judge James E. Boasberg
      year: 2025
      finding: 'The FTC failed to prove Meta holds monopoly power now: TikTok and YouTube belong in the market for users'' time. The FTC had alleged Meta bought Instagram (2012) and WhatsApp (2014) to neutralise rising rivals.'
    - id: mehta2025
      title: United States v. Google LLC, Memorandum Opinion on Remedies (D.D.C., 2 September 2025)
      authors: Judge Amit P. Mehta
      year: 2025
      finding: Declined to order Google to divest Chrome or Android; barred exclusive distribution contracts for Search, Chrome, Assistant and Gemini; required some data sharing with qualified competitors; cited generative AI as a new competitive force.

