    text meta "Meta, 2025:" cites ["boasberg2025"] x 790 y 540 font_size 36 hidden true
    text meta2 "not a monopoly\n(TikTok, YouTube)" cites ["boasberg2025"] x 790 y 455 font_size 34 hidden true
    text meta_but "bought Instagram,\nWhatsApp small" #b5482e cites ["boasberg2025"] x 790 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    text goog "Google, 2025:" cites ["mehta2025"] x 790 y 540 font_size 36 hidden true
    text goog2 "monopolist,\nkeeps Chrome" cites ["mehta2025"] x 790 y 455 font_size 34 hidden true
    text goog_rule "no exclusive\ndefault deals" #2f6b8f cites ["mehta2025"] x 790 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true
    text sure1 "Zero price can\nhide harm:\nmostly agreed" x 790 y 500 font_size 36 hidden true
    text sure2 "Break up or\nopen up:\ncontested" #b5482e x 790 y 300 font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch close work ["guard prices only → free is innocent","guard the way in → count the network"] x 500 y 420 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Break up, or open up?"

So the case for a breakup hangs on one thing: how much of the network a split would destroy.
    draw base lbl1 lbl2 bar1 val1 bar2 val2 cap

B: And that bar assumes a split halves it. If buyers use both apps, or (@draw both@) the apps must connect, the network survives.
    animate h 1 duration 2.5 ghost true

That's the bet behind Europe's (@draw eu@) Digital Markets Act: since 2024 the biggest platforms must let rivals connect, and may not favour their own services.

    erase eu bar2_ghost

Rivals can also arrive on their own.  (@draw meta@)In 2025 a judge ruled that Meta isn't a monopoly at (@draw meta2@) all, because TikTok and YouTube compete for the same time.

B: Critics answer that Meta bought Inst (@draw meta_but@)agram and WhatsApp while they were small, so they would never grow into that rival.

    erase meta meta2 meta_but

And Google? Th (@draw goog@)e judge who called it a monopolist refused (@draw goog2@) in 2025 to make it sell Chrome, partly because AI chatbots now compete with search.

B: So the court found the power, then chose rules over a  (@draw goog_rule@)breakup: no more exclusive deals for the default spot.

    erase goog goog2 goog_rule

How sure are we? That (@draw sure1@) a zero price can hide real harm: mostly agreed.

B: Whether breaking up bea (@draw sure2@)ts opening up: contested. There is almost no evidence yet on what splitting a platform does.

    pause 0.5

    erase sure1 sure2

    erase base lbl1 lbl2 bar1 val1 bar2 val2 both cap

    quiz "A free map app with 95% of users doubles its ads, and almost nobody leaves. What does a price-only test conclude?" intro "Three new cases." reveal "No harm, by price alone"
        right: No harm: the price-only test sees zero before and after. Count the extra ads as a price, and it went up.
        wrong: Ask what number a price-only test looks at. It sees zero before and after: here, no harm by that test.
        + No harm: the price is still zero
        * Harm: the price doubled
        * Harm: ads are illegal

    quiz "A ride app charges drivers no commission for a year to sign them up. Is that clearly predatory?" reveal "Not by itself" voice b
        right: Not by itself: a platform often prices one side low to win the other. It needs a closer look, not an automatic verdict.
        wrong: Think of the fishers' app, where one side paid nothing on purpose: here, not by itself.
        * Yes: it's below cost
        + Not by itself: platforms often price one side low
        * No: a price can never be predatory

    quiz "A city's biggest food-delivery app has most of its diners and restaurants. Which remedy keeps that network but adds competition?"
        right: Let rival apps take its diners' orders: every restaurant stays in reach of every diner, and the commission has to compete.
        wrong: Which option keeps every restaurant in reach of every diner? Here, let rival apps take orders from its diners.
        reveal: Let rivals take its orders
        * Split it into two delivery apps
        + Make it let rival apps take orders from its diners
        * Cap how many restaurants it lists

So should big tech be broken up? If antitrust guards onl (@draw close_box close_line_1@)y prices, a free platform is innocent. If it guards the way in for rivals,  (@draw close_line_2@)the answer turns on how much network a split would cost.

    pause 0.8

## Where next
